===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*teng-|id=think}}
From {{inh|en|enm|thinken}}, {{m|enm|thynken}}, {{m|enm|thenken}}, {{m|enm|thenchen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|þenċan}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*þankijan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*þankijaną||to think}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*teng-||to think, feel, know|id=think}}.

Cognate with {{cog|sco|think}}, {{m|sco|thynk||to think}}, {{cog|frr|teenk}}, {{m|frr|taanke}}, {{m|frr|tanke}}, {{m|frr|tånke||to think}}, {{cog|stq|toanke||to think}}, {{cog|fy|tinke||to think}}, {{cog|nl|denken||to think}}, {{cog|af|dink||to think}}, {{cog|nds|denken|}}, {{m|nds|dinken||to think}}, {{cog|de|denken||to think}}, {{cog|da|tænke||to think}}, {{cog|sv|tänka||to think}}, {{cog|nb|tenke||to think}}, {{cog|nn|tenkja||to think}}, {{cog|is|þekkja||to know, recognise, identify, perceive}}, {{cog|la|tongeō||know}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|thinks|thinking|thought}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[ponder]], to go over in one's head.
#: {{ux|en|Idly, the detective '''thought''' what his next move should be.}}
#* {{RQ:Ferguson Zollenstein|IV
|passage=So this was my future home, I '''thought'''! Certainly it made a brave picture. I had seen similar ones fired-in on many a Heidelberg stein. Backed by towering hills,{{...}}a sky of palest Gobelin flecked with fat, fleecy little clouds, it in truth looked a dear little city; the city of one's dreams.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To communicate to oneself in one's mind, to try to find a solution to a problem.
#: {{ux|en|I '''thought''' for three hours about the problem and still couldn’t find the solution.}}
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=1|passage='''Thinks''' I to myself, &ldquo;Sol, you're run off your course again. This is a rich man's summer &lsquo;cottage&rsquo; and if you don't look out there's likely to be some nice, lively dog taking an interest in your underpinning.&rdquo;}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[conceive]] of something or someone ''(usually followed by '''of'''; infrequently, by '''on''').''
#: {{ux|en|I tend to '''think''' of her as rather ugly.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-08-03|volume=408|issue=8847|magazine=w:The Economist
|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/finance-and-economics/21582512-explosion-start-ups-changing-finance-better-revenge-nerds Revenge of the nerds]
|passage='''Think''' of banking today and the image is of grey-suited men in towering skyscrapers. Its future, however, is being shaped in converted warehouses and funky offices in San Francisco, New York and London, where bright young things in jeans and T-shirts huddle around laptops, sipping lattes or munching on free food.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To be of [[opinion]] (that); to [[consider]], [[judge]], [[regard]], or [[look upon]] (something) as.
#: {{ux|en|At the time I '''thought''' his adamant refusal to give in right.}}
#: {{ux|en|I hope you won’t '''think''' me stupid if I ask you what that means.}}
#: {{ux|en|I '''think''' she is pretty, contrary to most people.}}
#: {{ux|en|Boxing is '''thought''' to be a dangerous sport.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Twelfth Night|I|ii|255|column=2|passage=My brother he is in Elizium, / Perchance he is not drown'd: What '''thinke''' you, ſaylors?.}}
#* '''1865''', Henry David Thoreau, ''Cape Cod'', Chapter IX. "The Sea and the Desert", page 182.
#*: {{...|There were a few trees among the houses, especially silver abeles, willows, and balm-of-Gileads; and}} one man showed me a young oak which he had transplanted from behind the town, '''thinking''' it an apple-tree.
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=3|passage=Now all this was very fine, but not at all in keeping with the Celebrity's character as I had come to conceive it. The idea that adulation ever cloyed on him was ludicrous in itself. In fact I '''thought''' the whole story fishy, and came very near to saying so.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[guess]]; to [[reckon]].
#: {{ux|en|I '''think''' she’ll pass the examination.}}
# To [[plan]]; to be [[consider]]ing; to be of a [[mind]] (to do something).
#* {{RQ:Scott Ivanhoe
|passage=The cupbearer shrugged up his shoulders in displeasure. "I '''thought''' to have lodged him in the solere chamber," said he{{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=4|passage=“Well,” I answered, at first with uncertainty, then with inspiration, “he would do splendidly to lead your cotillon, if you '''think''' of having one.” &para; “So you do not dance, Mr. Crocker?” &para; I was somewhat set back by her perspicuity.}}
# To presume; to venture.
#* {{RQ:KJV|Matthew|3|9|passage='''Think''' not to say within yourselves, We have Abraham to our father.}}
# {{lb|en|informal|used to show obviousness or agreement}} {{ellipsis of|en|[[think]] [[so]]}}
#: {{usex|en|These plants are dead.<br>Uh, you '''think'''?}}

