==Scots==

===Etymology===
From earlier {{m|sco|thynk}}, from {{inh|sco|enm|thynken}}, {{m|enm|thinken}}, from {{inh|sco|ang|þencan}}, {{m|ang|þenċean}}.

===Verb===
{{sco-verb|thinks|thinking|thocht}}

# {{lb|sco|transitive}} to {{l|en|think}}, to [[conceive]], to have in [[mind]]
# {{lb|sco|transitive}} to [[believe]], to hold as an [[opinion]], to [[judge]]; to [[feel]], to have as an [[emotion]]
#* {{quote-book|sco|year=1895|author=w:Ian Maclaren|title=A Doctor of the Old School|page=175|text=He hed juist ae faut, tae ma '''thinkin''', for a’ never jidged the waur o’ him for his titch of rochness—guid trees hae gnarled bark—but he '''thotched''' ower little o’ himsel’.|translation=He had just one fault, in my '''opinion''', for I never judged the worse of him for his touch of roughness—good trees have gnarled bark—but he '''thought''' over little of himself.}}
# {{lb|sco|transitive|or|intransitive}} to [[ponder]], to [[meditate]], to [[consider]], to [[reflect]] on
# {{lb|sco|transitive|or|intransitive}} to have [[scruple]]s, to [[doubt]], to [[reconsider]]
#* {{quote-book|sco|year=1924|author=Marion Angus|title=Tinker's road, and other verses|page=37|chapter=Think Lang|passage=Lassie, '''think''' lang, '''think''' lang, / Ere his step comes ower the hill. / Luve gi’es wi’ a launch an’ a sang, / An’ whiles for nocht bit ill.|translation=Girl, '''think''' more, '''think''' more, before his step comes over the hill. Love comes with a laugh and a song, and sometimes for nothing but harm.}}
# to [[devise]], to work out, to [[contrive]]
# {{lb|sco|archaic|with {{l|sco|shame}}}} to be [[ashamed]]
#* {{quote-book|sco|year=1853|author=David Macbeth Moir|title=The Life of Mansie Wauch|page=225|text='''Think''' shame—'''think''' shame—'''think''' black-burning shame o’ yoursell, ye born and bred ruffian!|translation='''Be ashamed'''—'''be ashamed'''—'''be''' deeply '''ashamed''' of yourself, you born and bred ruffian!}}

